  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft looks to bring general mild and dry conditions for \nmuch of the upcoming workweek; however, there will be a weak \nfrontal passage that brings a cooler low level easterly flow \ntonight into Wednesday. The effects of this will be mostly felt \nover the plains where there looks to be some nighttime and early \nmorning patchy fog development and a slight cooldown. There will \nalso be some patchy fog over some of the southwest valleys during \nthis time, especially the colder high valleys along the MT/ID/WY \nborders. Otherwise, temperatures will mostly run a few degrees \nabove average for most locations and there will be localized \nbreeziness.\n\nStrong westerly flow aloft becomes established over the Northern \nRockies during the second half of the week. The first of a series \nof fast moving zonal shortwaves will bring an initial round of \nbreezy to windy conditions Thursday into Friday. This will be \nfollowed by additional systems parading through the forecast area \nduring the weekend. Ensembles are having some difficulty resolving\nthe timing and strength of each system, but they do agree that \nthe Rocky Mountain Front and plains will be the recipients of the \nstrongest winds. With ridging generally remaining the most \ninfluential weather feature, despite the elevated westerly flow \nand shortwave passages, temperatures look to remain above average \nand precipitation will generally be spotty and confined to \nmountain areas. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nPatchy Fog Wednesday morning\u2026\n\nLatest Hi-Res Ensemble Forecast (HREF) guidance supports a low, \n10-30%, chance for visibilities to fall below one quarter of a \nmile along the Hi-Line from Cut Bank points east through Blaine \nCounty from midnight tonight through late Wednesday morning. The \nprobabilities are highest in the Milk River Valley from Fresno \nReservoir points northwest into southern Alberta. BUFKIT soundings \nfor locations along the Hi-Line do support the potential for fog \ngiven very dry air just above the boundary layer and light winds \nwithin the boundary layer; however, current thinking is that fog \nwill be patchy in nature, with the densest fog being located near \nlocal waterways (i.e. Milk River, Marias River, Fresno Reservoir, \nLake Elwell). Given the expected patchy nature of the densest fog \nwe are currently not anticipating the need for any Dense Fog \nAdvisories, but will continue to monitor the situation. - Moldan\n\nHigh Winds Possible at Times from Thursday Through the Weekend\u2026\nUncertainty still exists with the timing of individual waves and \nwhere the core of strongest mid-level winds will be throughout the \ntimeframe, with a significant spread in wind gusts between the \nensemble members of the GFS (stronger) and ECMWF (weaker). With \nthat being said, NBM probabilities continue to support the periods\nfrom Thursday evening through Friday evening and then from Sunday\nmorning through Sunday evening for the potential of high winds, \nmost notably along the Rocky Mountain Front.",
